# Line endings, declared once so every machine agrees instead of each one
# deciding on checkout.
#
# The problem this fixes: `core.autocrlf=true` is the default on Windows, so a
# checkout there rewrites LF to CRLF in the working copy. Prettier expects LF,
# and `prettier --check` is a gate in `pnpm run validate`, which CI runs on every
# push. On Linux CI the files are LF and pass; on a Windows machine the same
# files are CRLF and 67 of them fail. The code is identical in both places.
#
# The repository already stores LF. This does not rewrite history or committed
# content: it stops the checkout from converting, so the working copy matches
# what is stored. Expect no content diff from adding it.
#
# After changing this file, the working copy has to be refreshed for the new
# attributes to take effect - git will not see already-checked-out files as
# modified. Removing and restoring the tracked files does it:
#
#     git ls-files -z | xargs -0 rm -f
#     git checkout -- .
#
# LF everywhere is the right default here: Python, TypeScript and Markdown all
# take it, and no tracked file needs CRLF (a .bat or .cmd would, and there are
# none). `text=auto` still lets git detect binary files and leave them alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the one tracked binary, and for formats likely to arrive
# later. A misdetected binary gets corrupted by line-ending conversion, and the
# corruption is silent.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.lance binary
